'''Banc de l'Estret, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Banc de l'Estret}}; "Bank of the Estret") is a defunct Balboan regional bank that operated in the [[Estret Province|Estret]] corridor from 1962 until its failure in 1988. The bank was one of two smaller financial institutions that collapsed during the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]], after expanding aggressively into property finance during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble]]. Its former headquarters, the [[Torre FDB]] at 200 Carrer Miquel in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] of [[Sant Cristòfor]], was subsequently acquired by the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]].

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1962–1978) ===

Banc de l'Estret was founded in 1962 by [[Ramon Vidal i Capdevila]], a Sant Cristòfor-based banker who had previously managed a private lending business serving the commercial merchants and small manufacturers of the Estret corridor. The bank was established shortly after the return of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|AC mandate]] territory to Balboa, when the integration of the Sant Cristòfor financial system into the national economy created opportunities for new entrants in the regional banking market.

Vidal i Capdevila positioned Banc de l'Estret as a regional alternative to [[Banc Balboa]], the dominant financial institution in the corridor. The bank's early business was concentrated in commercial lending to the trading houses, shipping firms, and small manufacturers of Sant Cristòfor and [[Portnou]], and in residential mortgage lending to the growing middle class of the Estret corridor. The bank operated a modest branch network of six offices across Sant Cristòfor and Portnou, and maintained a conservative lending posture throughout its first decade and a half.

By 1978, Banc de l'Estret had approximately $800 million in assets and was considered a well-managed but unremarkable regional institution. Its loan portfolio was diversified across commercial lending, trade finance, and residential mortgages, with limited exposure to speculative real estate development.

=== Expansion during the bubble (1978–1987) ===

The liberalisation of foreign real-estate ownership by the [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] government in the mid-1970s transformed the Estret corridor's property market and created the conditions for the [[Balboa real-estate bubble]]. Banc de l'Estret, like the other domestic banks, came under competitive pressure from [[Banc Balboa]], which was aggressively expanding its lending to foreign investors purchasing property in Sant Cristòfor and Portnou. Vidal i Capdevila, who had been cautious in the bank's first decade and a half, concluded that the regional bank could not remain on the sidelines of the boom without ceding market share to its larger competitor.

Beginning in approximately 1980, Banc de l'Estret shifted its lending strategy. The bank expanded its property finance division, offering commercial real-estate loans to domestic and foreign investors on terms that matched Banc Balboa's absorbent rates. It also began financing speculative construction projects in the [[Districte Financer (Original, Sant Cristòfor)|Districte Financer]] and along the Portnou waterfront, areas where property values were rising rapidly. The bank's loan portfolio shifted from a diversified mix of commercial, trade, and residential lending to a concentration in property finance: by 1985, approximately 60 per cent of the bank's loan book was exposed to real-estate development and commercial property investment.

The bank's rapid expansion was reflected in its physical presence. In 1981, Banc de l'Estret commissioned a new headquarters tower at 200 Carrer Miquel in the Districte Financer — a 41-storey building that would become one of the taller towers on the Sant Cristòfor waterfront. The [[Torre FDB]], as it is now known, was completed in 1983 and was intended to signal the bank's ambition to compete with the larger institutions concentrated along the waterfront. The bank also expanded its branch network to 14 offices across the Estret corridor.

By 1987, at the peak of the bubble, Banc de l'Estret had approximately $3.2 billion in assets — nearly four times its 1978 size — with the growth driven almost entirely by the expansion of its property finance portfolio. The bank's lending standards had eroded significantly: loans were being approved on the basis of projected property appreciation rather than borrower creditworthiness, and the collateral valuations were based on the inflated market prices of the bubble.

=== Collapse (1987–1988) ===

{{main|Balboa real-estate asset crisis}}

The bubble began to deflate in 1987, as rising interest rates in Illypnia and Fosia reduced the flow of foreign capital to emerging markets. Several large foreign investors defaulted on their loans from Banc Balboa, and the resulting foreclosures triggered a sharp decline in property values across the Estret corridor. The collapse in collateral values spread rapidly through the financial system, as the smaller institutions that had followed Banc Balboa's lending practices disclosed their own non-performing loans.

Banc de l'Estret was particularly exposed. The bank's concentrated property finance portfolio meant that the decline in commercial property values had an immediate and severe impact on its balance sheet. As property values fell, the collateral underlying the bank's loans proved insufficient to cover the outstanding debt, and the bank's provisions for loan losses were quickly overwhelmed. Depositors, aware of the bank's exposure, began withdrawing funds in early 1988.

By mid-1988, Banc de l'Estret was unable to meet its obligations. The [[Balboa Financial Services Authority]] placed the bank under administration, and its assets — including the headquarters tower — were placed under the control of the restructuring authorities. The bank was one of two smaller institutions that failed during the crisis; the other has not been publicly identified in the restructuring records.

=== Aftermath ===

The bank's assets were liquidated or transferred to other institutions under the supervision of the restructuring authorities and the [[Adonian Community Monetary Fund]] (ACMF). The bank's commercial loan portfolio was acquired by [[Banc de l'Istme]], which had maintained a conservative lending posture during the bubble and was in a position to absorb the distressed assets at favourable prices. The residential mortgage portfolio was transferred to [[Casa Balboa]], which was itself under government control following its seizure in 1989.

The headquarters tower at 200 Carrer Miquel remained under the control of the restructuring authorities until 1994, when it was acquired at a significant discount by the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]], the newly established sovereign wealth fund. The Fons established its headquarters in the building, which was subsequently renamed the [[Torre FDB]].

[[Ramon Vidal i Capdevila]] was not prosecuted in connection with the bank's failure. The restructuring authorities concluded that the bank's collapse was the result of poor judgement in matching Banc Balboa's lending practices rather than fraud, and Vidal i Capdevila cooperated with the investigation. He retired from public life and died in 1995.

